2186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1061 653769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32f48700-2e28-53f4-8117-05414a51c990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1061 653770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03cdecc5-044c-525b-9d1a-1f3234b5c9a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1061 653774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c84a2326-edc8-5f45-9916-727883af7cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079508 #edb/ref 1061 653778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079508 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01600498-e551-5c5b-b3e4-ea3cc665f3c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079508 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1064 "When simplifying expressions, we prefer not to leave a radical in the denominator. To eliminate a radical in an expression's denominator, we can use a process known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the radical that we wish to remove from the denominator.\n\nFor instance, to rationalize the denominator in the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nall we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the final expression no longer has a radical in the denominator. Thus, the denominator has been rationalized.\n\n**Note:** Because we multiplied the numerator and denominator by the same amount, the resulting expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}$ is equivalent to the original fraction $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ They are equivalent, even though they are written differently." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1065 1614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "17e8e5bd-0b03-5315-8d7a-fc97948fb0d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
